## Test Helpers

When tests have repetitive assertion logic, we can extract it into **test helper** functions. Helper functions don't run tests directly — they assist other tests.

## The Problem Without t.Helper()

Without `t.Helper()`, error line numbers point to the helper function, not where the helper was called:

```go
// Without t.Helper() — wrong line number!
func assertEqual(t *testing.T, got, want int) {
	if got != want {
		t.Errorf("got %d, want %d", got, want) // this line is shown
	}
}
```

## With t.Helper()

```go
// With t.Helper() — correct line number!
func assertEqual(t *testing.T, got, want int) {
	t.Helper() // mark this function as a helper
	if got != want {
		t.Errorf("got %d, want %d", got, want) // points to the caller
	}
}

func TestSomething(t *testing.T) {
	result := add(2, 3)
	assertEqual(t, result, 5) // <-- error will point to this line
}
```

## Common Helper Assertions

```go
package testutil

import (
	"testing"
	"reflect"
)

func AssertEqual(t *testing.T, got, want interface{}) {
	t.Helper()
	if !reflect.DeepEqual(got, want) {
		t.Errorf("\ngot:  %+v\nwant: %+v", got, want)
	}
}

func AssertNoError(t *testing.T, err error) {
	t.Helper()
	if err != nil {
		t.Fatalf("unexpected error: %v", err)
	}
}

func AssertError(t *testing.T, err error) {
	t.Helper()
	if err == nil {
		t.Fatal("expected an error but got nil")
	}
}
```

## Setup & Teardown with TestMain

```go
package mypackage

import (
	"fmt"
	"os"
	"testing"
)

func TestMain(m *testing.M) {
	// Setup before all tests
	fmt.Println("Setting up test environment...")
	setupDB()

	// Run all tests
	code := m.Run()

	// Teardown after all tests
	fmt.Println("Tearing down...")
	cleanupDB()

	os.Exit(code)
}
```

## Temporary Files in Tests

```go
func TestReadFile(t *testing.T) {
	// Create a temp file that is cleaned up automatically
	tmpFile, err := os.CreateTemp("", "test*.txt")
	if err != nil {
		t.Fatal(err)
	}
	t.Cleanup(func() { os.Remove(tmpFile.Name()) })

	tmpFile.WriteString("test content")
	tmpFile.Close()

	// Test using tmpFile.Name()
}
```

